// 08 · reasons — inference that keeps walking, and remembers why
//
// Mentl's inference is Hindley-Milner, with two properties worth the last file
// of this tour. First, it is productive under error: a type conflict does not
// stop the compile. The medium drops an error node at the conflict and keeps
// going, so the rest of the program still typechecks and the healthy parts
// still emit. You get every real diagnostic at once, not the first one and a
// wall.
//
// Second, every edge it draws carries a Reason. A type was not merely inferred;
// it was inferred BECAUSE — a literal here, a unification there, an install up
// the chain. The Why walk follows that chain back to where a fact was minted.
// No fact in the medium is asserted; every one is walkable.
//
// You never call the Why engine from your program — it is the medium reading
// itself. Point the cursor at a value and the medium answers:
//
//   mentl lib/tutorial/08-reasons.mn:35:4
//     Query: fn caller(x) = base(x) + 1 : (x: Int own — inferred) -> Int
//     Why:   ownership-resolved params of 'caller', at tutorial/08-reasons:35
//
// Or open the IDE, click any fact in the aspect ring, and the Reason draws.
//
// Run it:   mentl run  lib/tutorial/08-reasons.mn   ← prints 9
import io

// A clean binding. Its type and its row each carry a Reason edge, and the Why
// walk at this line reads them back to the literal and the unification that set
// them.
fn base(x) = x + 1

// caller typechecks even when a sibling has a type error: a conflict is
// isolated to its own node, so base and caller are unaffected, and the build
// still emits WAT for everything sound while reporting what is not.
fn caller(x) = base(x) + 1

fn main() = caller(7) |> int_to_str |> print_string
